[A1b WHAT omega IS — the containment theorem, round-1 MAJOR 2]
  THEOREM (of the committed SKY-B definition).  For a cover pair
  e < e':  if h[e'] - h[e] = 1 then D_e'(d-1) SUBSET D_e(d), so
  omega(e,e') = |D_e'(d-1)| / |D_e(d)|; otherwise the two sets are
  DISJOINT and omega = 0 identically.
  Proof.  D_e(d) = {f : e < f, h[f] = h[e]+d} and D_e'(d-1) =
  {f : e' < f, h[f] = h[e']+d-1}.  If h[e'] = h[e]+1 the height
  conditions coincide and e < e' < f implies e < f; otherwise the
  height conditions are incompatible.  []
  CONSEQUENCES, both against the pin's framing: the intersection
  is ALWAYS the whole successor chart, so 'the transition is the
  identity on their intersection' has nothing to act on; and
  omega is a CHART-SIZE RATIO along covers, which systematically
  favours THIN-charted records (a 2-direction chart reaches high
  omega when a neighbour retains 2; a 15-direction chart needs 15).
  [PASS] A1b [THEOREM, verified] omega IS A CHART-SIZE RATIO, NOT AN OVERLAP.  Both clauses of the containment theorem hold with ZERO violations over every cover pair of every record measured here, and — independently — the Jaccard index |A&B|/|A|B| equals omega to the last Fraction on all of them, which is only possible under containment.  The instrument's second leg must be described as what it is  (cover pairs checked = 14818; containment violations = 0; Jaccard-vs-omega disagreements = 0)

